WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.830
When it came to concealing his troubles, Tommy Wilhelm was not less capable than the next fellow.
说到掩饰自己的烦恼，汤米·威廉的本事并不比旁人差。

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:11.810
So at least he thought, and there was a certain amount of evidence to back him up.
至少他自己是这么想的，而且也有一定的证据可以支持他这个看法。

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:17.870
He had once been an actor—no, not quite, an extra—and he knew what acting should be.
他曾经当过演员——不，不完全是，是当过临时演员——他知道表演应该是什么样子。

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:27.840
Also, he was smoking a cigar, and when a man is smoking a cigar, wearing a hat, he has an advantage; it is harder to find out how he feels.
再说，他正抽着雪茄；一个人抽着雪茄、戴着帽子，就占了优势：别人更难弄清他心里是什么感受。

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:39.900
He came from the twenty-third floor down to the lobby on the mezzanine to collect his mail before breakfast, and he believed—he hoped—that he looked passably well: doing all right.
早饭前，他从二十三楼下到夹层的大堂去取信件；他相信——他希望——自己看上去还过得去：一副日子过得还不错的样子。

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:46.000
It was a matter of sheer hope, because there was not much that he could add to his present effort.
这全凭希望，因为在眼下这番努力之上，他再也添不了多少了。

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.830
On the fourteenth floor he looked for his father to enter the elevator; they often met at this hour, on the way to breakfast.
到了十四楼，他盼着父亲走进电梯来；父子俩常在这个钟点、在去吃早饭的路上碰面。

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.530
If he worried about his appearance it was mainly for his old father’s sake.
他若是为自己的外表操心，那主要是为了年迈的父亲。

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:03.400
But there was no stop on the fourteenth, and the elevator sank and sank.
可电梯在十四楼没有停，一直往下沉，往下沉。

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:10.790
Then the smooth door opened and the great dark-red uneven carpet that covered the lobby billowed toward Wilhelm’s feet.
接着，光滑的电梯门开了，那块铺满大堂、凹凸不平的深红色大地毯朝威廉脚下翻涌过来。

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.660
In the foreground the lobby was dark, sleepy.
近处的大堂光线昏暗，睡意沉沉。

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:28.260
French drapes like sails kept out the sun, but three high, narrow windows were open, and in the blue air Wilhelm saw a pigeon about to light on the great chain that supported the marquee of the movie house directly underneath the lobby.
船帆似的法式帷幔挡住了阳光，但有三扇又高又窄的窗户开着；在湛蓝的空中，威廉看见一只鸽子正要落到那根粗大的链条上，链条吊着大堂正下方那家电影院的遮檐。

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.790
For one moment he heard the wings beating strongly.
有那么一会儿，他听见那对翅膀在有力地扑打。

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:37.070
Most of the guests at the Hotel Gloriana were past the age of retirement.
格洛丽亚纳饭店的住客大多已过了退休年龄。

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:45.280
Along Broadway in the Seventies, Eighties, and Nineties, a great part of New York’s vast population of old men and women lives.
纽约数量庞大的老年男女，有很大一部分住在百老汇大街沿线的七十几街、八十几街和九十几街一带。

00:01:46.080 --> 00:02:03.080
Unless the weather is too cold or wet they fill the benches about the tiny railed parks and along the subway gratings from Verdi Square to Columbia University, they crowd the shops and cafeterias, the dime stores, the tearooms, the bakeries, the beauty parlors, the reading rooms and club rooms.
只要天气不太冷、雨不太大，从威尔第广场到哥伦比亚大学，那些围着栏杆的小小公园四周和地铁通风栅栏沿线的长椅上就坐满了他们；他们挤满了商店和自助餐馆、廉价杂货店、茶室、面包店、美容院、阅览室和俱乐部活动室。

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.910
Among these old people at the Gloriana, Wilhelm felt out of place.
置身于格洛丽亚纳饭店的这些老人之中，威廉觉得自己格格不入。

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:19.000
He was comparatively young, in his middle forties, large and blond, with big shoulders; his back was heavy and strong, if already a little stooped or thickened.
他相对年轻，四十五岁上下，身材高大，一头金发，肩膀宽阔；他的背厚实强壮，只是已经有点驼了，或者说有点变厚了。

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:30.400
After breakfast the old guests sat down on the green leather armchairs and sofas in the lobby and began to gossip and look into the papers; they had nothing to do but wait out the day.
早饭后，那些上了年纪的住客在大堂的绿色皮扶手椅和沙发上坐下，开始闲聊、翻看报纸；他们无事可做，只能熬过这一天。

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.910
But Wilhelm was used to an active life and liked to go out energetically in the morning.
但威廉过惯了忙碌的生活，喜欢一早就精神抖擞地出门。

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:45.370
And for several months, because he had no position, he had kept up his morale by rising early; he was shaved and in the lobby by eight o’clock.
而且，几个月来，由于没有工作，他一直靠早起来维持士气；八点钟以前，他就已经刮好胡子，到了大堂。

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:52.290
He bought the paper and some cigars and drank a Coca-Cola or two before he went in to breakfast with his father.
他会买份报纸、几支雪茄，喝上一两瓶可口可乐，然后才进去和父亲一起吃早饭。

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:57.020
After breakfast—out, out, out to attend to business.
早饭以后——出门，出门，出门去办事。

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.730
The getting out had in itself become the chief business.
出门这件事本身，已经成了头等大事。

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:06.490
But he had realized that he could not keep this up much longer, and today he was afraid.
但他已经意识到，自己没法把这种日子再维持多久了，而今天他感到害怕。

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:14.940
He was aware that his routine was about to break up and he sensed that a huge trouble long presaged but till now formless was due.
他心里清楚，自己的日常惯例就要瓦解了；他感觉到，一场早有预感、但至今还没有成形的大麻烦就要来了。

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.780
Before evening, he’d know.
不到傍晚，他就会知道。

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.980
Nevertheless he followed his daily course and crossed the lobby.
尽管如此，他还是照每天的惯例，穿过了大堂。

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.880
Rubin, the man at the newsstand, had poor eyes.
报摊的鲁宾眼睛不好。

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:32.990
They may not have been actually weak but they were poor in expression, with lacy lids that furled down at the corners.
那双眼睛也许并不是真的视力差，但缺乏神采，眼皮皱得像花边，在眼角处向下卷着。

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:34.830
He dressed well.
他衣着讲究。

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:41.430
It didn’t seem necessary—he was behind the counter most of the time—but he dressed very well.
这似乎没有必要——他大部分时间都待在柜台后面——可他还是穿得非常讲究。

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:46.640
He had on a rich brown suit; the cuffs embarrassed the hairs on his small hands.
他穿着一套浓褐色的西装；袖口让他那双小手上的汗毛都难为情了。

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.670
He wore a Countess Mara painted necktie.
他系着一条玛拉伯爵夫人牌的手绘领带。

00:03:50.470 --> 00:04:00.330
As Wilhelm approached, Rubin did not see him; he was looking out dreamily at the Hotel Ansonia, which was visible from his corner, several blocks away.
威廉走过来时，鲁宾没有看见他；鲁宾正出神地向外望着安索尼亚饭店，那座饭店在几个街区以外，从他那个角落望得见。

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:06.110
The Ansonia, the neighborhood’s great landmark, was built by Stanford White.
安索尼亚饭店是这一带的著名地标，出自斯坦福·怀特之手。

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:18.570
It looks like a baroque palace from Prague or Munich enlarged a hundred times, with towers, domes, huge swells and bubbles of metal gone green from exposure, iron fretwork and festoons.
它看上去就像把布拉格或慕尼黑的一座巴洛克式宫殿放大了一百倍，有塔楼，有穹顶，有因风吹日晒而发绿的巨大金属凸起和圆泡，还有铁制的回纹装饰和花彩。

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:23.300
Black television antennae are densely planted on its round summits.
在它那一个个圆圆的顶端上，密密麻麻地竖着黑色的电视天线。

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:31.800
Under the changes of weather, it may look like marble or like sea water, black as slate in the fog, white as tufa in sunlight.
随着天气变化，它看上去可能像大理石，也可能像海水；雾中黑如石板，阳光下白如石灰华。

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:40.750
This morning it looked like the image of itself reflected in deep water, white and cumulous above, with cavernous distortions underneath.
今天早上，它就像自己倒映在深水里的影子，上部洁白，如积云层层堆叠，下部则扭曲变形，如洞穴般幽深。

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.310
Together, the two men gazed at it.
两个人一起凝望着它。

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:49.100
Then Rubin said, “Your dad is in to breakfast already, the old gentleman.”
接着鲁宾说：“你爸已经进去吃早饭了，那位老先生。”

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:50.730
“Oh, yes?
“哦，是吗？

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:52.750
Ahead of me today?”
今天比我还早？”

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:56.280
“That’s a real knocked-out shirt you got on,” said Rubin.
鲁宾说：“你身上这件衬衫真是棒极了。”

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.970
“Where’s it from, Saks?”
“哪儿买的？萨克斯百货？”

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:02.330
“No, it’s a Jack Fagman—Chicago.”
“不是，是杰克·法格曼牌的——芝加哥的。”

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:08.030
Even when his spirits were low, Wilhelm could still wrinkle his forehead in a pleasing way.
即使在情绪低落的时候，威廉依然能把额头皱得讨人喜欢。

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:12.600
Some of the slow, silent movements of his face were very attractive.
他脸上有些缓慢、无声的动作十分迷人。

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.540
He went back a step, as if to stand away from himself and get a better look at his shirt.
他往后退了一步，仿佛要站得离自己远一点，好把身上的衬衫看得更清楚些。

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.890
His glance was comic, a comment upon his untidiness.
他的眼神很滑稽，是在评说自己的不修边幅。

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:28.900
He liked to wear good clothes, but once he had put it on each article appeared to go its own way.
他喜欢穿好衣服，可每件衣服一旦穿上了身，似乎就各行其是了。

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:38.720
Wilhelm, laughing, panted a little; his teeth were small; his cheeks when he laughed and puffed grew round, and he looked much younger than his years.
威廉笑着，有点气喘；他牙齿很小；一笑、一喘，脸颊就鼓得圆圆的，看上去比实际年龄年轻得多。

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:50.630
In the old days when he was a college freshman and wore a raccoon coat and a beanie on his large blond head his father used to say that, big as he was, he could charm a bird out of a tree.
当年他还是大学一年级新生，穿着浣熊皮大衣，大大的金发脑袋上戴一顶无檐小帽，那时他父亲常说，别看他个头这么大，却能把树上的鸟儿都哄下来。

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:53.050
Wilhelm had great charm still.
威廉现在依然魅力十足。

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:57.840
“I like this dove-gray color,” he said in his sociable, good-natured way.
他带着惯有的那股爱交际、好脾气的劲儿说：“我喜欢这种鸽灰色。”

00:05:58.640 --> 00:05:59.840
“It isn’t washable.
“这件不能水洗。

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:02.350
You have to send it to the cleaner.
你得送去干洗店。

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.170
It never smells as good as washed.
干洗出来，闻着从来都不如水洗的好闻。

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.210
But it’s a nice shirt.
不过这件衬衫是挺好的。

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.540
It cost sixteen, eighteen bucks.”
这件花了十六还是十八块钱。”
